<section class="prose section">
  <div class="container container--narrow">
    <p>
      Newsprint is softer and more absorbent than glossy paper, and some
      Canva settings can reduce the print quality of your ad. A few simple
      steps in Canva will make sure your ad looks as sharp as possible in print.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Set up your design</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Use the exact ad size we give you.</strong> Create a custom-size
        design in millimetres, matching your booked ad size (if in doubt, check with
        <a href="mailto:advertising@pctimes.co.nz">advertising@pctimes.co.nz</a>).</li>
      <li><strong>Keep a safe margin.</strong> Keep text, logos and contact details
        at least 3mm in from the edges.</li>
      <li><strong>Add a border if your ad has a white background,</strong> so it
        stands out from the editorial around it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. Text</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Black text should always be pure black:</strong> RGB 0, 0, 0
        (hex #000000). Don't use dark grey or "near black", as it can print fuzzy or patchy.</li>
      <li><strong>Keep text at least 7pt.</strong> Anything smaller can blur or fill in on newsprint.</li>
      <li><strong>Avoid very thin or delicate fonts,</strong> especially at small sizes.</li>
      <li><strong>Be careful with white text on dark or coloured backgrounds.</strong> Keep it large and bold.</li>
      <li><strong>Avoid small coloured text.</strong> Small text prints best in black.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>3. Colour and images</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Expect colours to print darker and less vivid</strong> than on
        your screen. Avoid very dark backgrounds with dark text or images on top.</li>
      <li><strong>Avoid very pale tints.</strong> Light pastel colours and tints
        under about 10% may disappear on newsprint.</li>
      <li><strong>Brighten your photos.</strong> Images print darker on newsprint,
        so lift the brightness slightly in Canva's photo editor.</li>
      <li><strong>Use good-quality images.</strong> Low-resolution photos or images
        copied from websites can look blurry or pixelated in print.</li>
      <li><strong>Make QR codes at least 2cm square,</strong> in black on a white background.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>4. Download from Canva</h2>
    <p>Click <strong>Share → Download</strong>, then choose:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>File type:</strong> PDF</li>
      <li><strong>Preset:</strong> Print</li>
      <li><strong>Colour profile:</strong> CMYK (best for professional printing), if you have Canva Pro</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Under <strong>Advanced Settings</strong>, make sure the following are all turned <strong>off</strong>:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Crop marks and bleed</li>
      <li>Flatten PDF</li>
      <li>Include notes</li>
      <li>Match reading order to layers</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>5. Check your PDF before you send it</h2>
    <p>
      Open your PDF in Adobe Acrobat (or the free Acrobat Reader) and go to
      <strong>File → Properties → Fonts</strong>.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>If you see a list of fonts,</strong> your text has been saved as
        live text, and your ad will probably print well.</li>
      <li><strong>If the list is empty,</strong> your text may have been turned into
        an image. Check that <em>Flatten PDF</em> was switched off and download it again.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Zoom in to check that text and logos look crisp, and proofread phone
      numbers, addresses, dates and prices one last time.
    </p>

